uestion be resolved by looking at something I have access to?** Repo conventions, existing tests, git history, the issue text, the docs. If yes, it's my call — go read more and decide, don't ask. If the answer lives in someone's head, in a conversation I wasn't in, or in a constraint nobody has written down, it's a human call no matter how long I reason about it.\n\nNote that this is *not* \"is it ambiguous.\" Most ambiguity dissolves on closer reading. The kind that doesn't is specifically about **absent** information, not unclear information. `slugify('Café')` is unresolvable not because the issue is vague but because whoever wrote it probably never considered it, and the right answer depends on things the repo can't tell me: whether slugs get persisted, whether there's an existing corpus, whether the product is localized.\n\n**Second axis: reversibility × blast radius.** Even resolvable questions change character when a wrong guess is expensive. Slug format looks like a trivia-level regex detail and is actually a one-way door — the moment slugs land in URLs or a database, changing the function silently breaks every existing link. Compare: regex vs. loop, test naming, file layout. Reversible in one commit, no external consumer, never worth asking about.\n\n**\"Done\" has layers, and people equivocate across them:**\n\n1. Tests pass — I can verify this alone.\n2. It does what was asked — verifiable only to the extent the ask was fully pinned.\n3. It solves the problem you actually had — I essentially cannot verify this alone, because an issue is a lossy compression of intent.\n\nI can honestly claim 1 and 2 *under stated assumptions*. Claiming 3 is usually overreach on my part.\n\n**One pushback, since it's the standard answer:** \"done = acceptance criteria met\" is close to circular. It relocates the judgment to whoever wrote the criteria — and writing good criteria *is* the judgment call. Useful discipline, not a solution.\n\n**And a bias worth naming, mine:** finishing is the rewarded state, so I'll drift toward declaring done. My confidence is reasonably calibrated on whether code does what I wrote it to do, and poorly calibrated on whether I understood what you wanted. The practical consequence is that \"not done, here's the open question\" needs to stay a cheap, normal report rather than a failure state.\n\nThe flip side is that questions are a budget. Asking costs a round-trip; asking too often trains you to skim my questions, which is worse than either guessing or asking. So I try to spend it only where the answer visibly changes the product — which is why I raised non-ASCII and non-string input but said nothing about test naming.\n\n**The working rule I'd propose:** if I can compress the assumption into one sentence a reasonable reader would either nod at or immediately object to, state it and proceed. If I catch myself writing a paragraph of justification, that length is the tell that the decision isn't mine.\n\nLast thing, specific to the TDD shape we're in: writing tests first is a spec-completeness detector. It forces fuzzy prose into explicit decisions and surfaces the unresolvable ones *before* implementation sinks cost into a guess. It's also why I left `'Café'` unasserted rather than picking one — a guess baked into a test is worse than one baked into implementation, because the test launders it into stated intent for whoever reads it next.\n\nWhere would you draw the line differently? I'm particularly curious whether you'd want me to default to deciding-and-flagging more aggressively than I did here.","ttft_ms":50986,"type":"result","duration_ms":69035,"uuid":"936a9509-4673-4e38-b240-de403fce811f","ttft_stream_ms":731,"time_to_request_ms":183,"first_content_frame_ms":731,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
